---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const roles = [
  { org: 'Nura', role: 'Backend architecture for an AI journaling app', when: '2025' },
  { org: 'Intosoft', role: 'Backend engineer on search, real-time, and billing', when: '2024–2025' },
  { org: 'Yatri Design Studio', role: 'Software engineer on EV charging admin and inventory', when: '2023–2024' },
  { org: 'Leapfrog Technology', role: 'Software engineer intern', when: '2022' },
]
---

<Base title="About" description="Bishal Hona, a backend engineer with about three years building Node and TypeScript systems, who builds for the failure case and the business case, not just the happy path.">
  <section class="shell ab-head">
    <p class="label">About</p>
    <h1 class="display ab-title measure">
      I build backend systems that hold up under real use, real load, and
      people trying to break them.
    </h1>
  </section>

  <section class="shell ab-body">
    <div class="ab-prose measure flow">
      <p>
        I've spent about three years writing Node and TypeScript backends, and
        the pattern that keeps repeating is: the feature someone asked for is
        rarely the hard part. The hard part is what happens around it. A job
        queue that has to keep running when one job in it fails badly. A
        session that has to mean the same thing to the database as it does to
        the person holding it. An endpoint that has to survive someone
        deliberately poking at it, not just a well-behaved client.
      </p>
      <p>
        I care about the business reasoning behind a decision almost as much
        as the decision itself. Every extra queue, every retry, every bit of
        auth complexity has a cost, in engineering time and in what it does to
        the product's speed. I'd rather ship the boring version that holds up
        than the clever one that needs a pager at 2am. And I think about the
        person on the other end of the request, because a backend that's
        technically correct but slow, or confusing, or quietly drops their
        data, has failed just as badly as one that's down.
      </p>
      <p>
        Security isn't a task I get to after the feature works. Tokens that
        rotate and get revoked properly, not just issued. Input I don't trust
        by default, wherever it enters the system. Permissions checked at the
        layer that actually enforces them, not the one that hides a button. It
        costs a bit more time up front, and I'd rather spend it than explain
        later why it didn't happen.
      </p>
      <p>
        I keep orchestration, external calls, and storage cleanly separated,
        mostly so I can reason about what happens when one of them fails
        before it does. AI tooling has made me faster at writing the code. It
        hasn't changed how I think about any of this, and I don't expect it
        to.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="ab-side">
      <p class="label ab-side__label">Background</p>
      <ul class="ab-timeline">
        {roles.map((r) => (
          <li class="ab-role">
            <span class="ab-role__when label">{r.when}</span>
            <span class="ab-role__org mono">{r.org}</span>
            <span class="ab-role__desc">{r.role}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <p class="label ab-side__label ab-side__label--2">Working with</p>
      <p class="ab-stack mono">
        Node · TypeScript · PostgreSQL · MongoDB · Redis · BullMQ · Socket.IO · Docker · AWS
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .ab-head { padding-top: clamp(2rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 4rem); }
  .ab-title {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    margin-top: 0.8rem;
  }

  .ab-body {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4.5rem);
    padding-bottom: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4.5rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.6fr 1fr;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 4vw, 5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .ab-prose p { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--graphite); }
  .ab-prose b { color: var(--ink); }

  .ab-side { position: sticky; top: 2rem; }
  .ab-side__label { display: block; margin-bottom: 1.1rem; }
  .ab-side__label--2 { margin-top: 2.4rem; }

  .ab-timeline {
    list-style: none; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column;
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .ab-role {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .ab-role:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  .ab-role__when { color: var(--rust); }
  .ab-role__org { color: var(--ink); font-size: var(--step-0); }
  .ab-role__desc { color: var(--graphite); font-size: var(--step--1); line-height: 1.45; }

  .ab-stack {
    color: var(--graphite); font-size: var(--step--1); line-height: 1.9;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--ink); background: var(--panel);
  }

  @media (max-width: 46rem) {
    .ab-body { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .ab-side { position: static; }
  }
</style>
